A1. This brought down the house out in California. ~ EARTHQUAKE
A10. A Saturday evening affair. ~ POST
A14. Where American tourists have their ups and downs. ~ ASEA
A15. The kind of wear one gets out of an overnight bag. ~ UNDER
A16. This goddess is responsible for all the moonshine. ~ LUNA
A17. A kind of degree. (You will get this after a second.) ~ THIRD
A19. What the world is going to become if the tooth paste ads continue. ~ USER
A20. This is sometimes seen puffing on a camel. ~ ARAB
A21. Methods used in making puzzles. ~ SYSTEMS
A23. Day-bite craving time. ~ NOON
A25. The way a lawyer starts pleading. ~ PL
A26. The foreigner that ate her affections. ~ ALIEN
A28. What he did the day he was married. ~ RUED
A30. Give it up. ~ SURRENDER
A34. What Cal will do for California. ~ STAND
A36. The part of the pantry that makes a good cook. ~ TRY
A37. When this starts snooping it doesn't get very far. ~ SN
A38. What men object to in the new gowns women are wearing. ~ COST
A40. This sounds sheepish when it broadcasts contempt. ~ BAH
A41. These letters might give you advertising benefits. ~ AB
A42. The poetic bucket. ~ OAKEN
A44. An introduction into society. ~ SO
A45. This is one on a Scotchman. ~ TAM
A47. What some one is sure to do to remind one of some boner. ~ TWIT
A48. If you wish to make the effort this will help some. ~ EF
A50. A Babe's plaything. ~ BAT
A51. There is no sense to this. ~ INANE
A53. The way a sportsman feels in evening clothes. ~ MISERABLE
A56. What an eraser is to a puzzle fan. ~ NEED
A58. The way the farmer hopes his corn crop will turn out. ~ EARED
A59. A popular word with Nanette. ~ NO
A61. This one helps to put the pep in Pepsodent. ~ AMOS
A63. A South American connection. ~ ISTHMUS
A67. What some people keep their radio going for. ~ EVER
A69. Something lucky to reach. ~ GOAL
A71. There must be a catch in this. ~ SEINE
A72. What King Carol expects to do now. ~ RULE
A73. A swanky home. ~ MANOR
A75. A line traveling men used to handle. ~ REIN
A76. This is not a line from an eye-testing card, but a character from Norse mythology. ~ YMIR
A77. On account of closing for the summer we are unable to give a key for these. ~ APARTMENTS

D1. What a fat man does for farm relief. ~ EATS
D2. The kind of a tip they let drop at the races. ~ ASHY
D3. A little change they found in Portugal. ~ REIS
D4. This one has a very bad temper; dentists try to get rid of it; and it often sauces some poor fish. ~ TARTAR
D5. This was passed as a dry measure but it might be good for the wets (abbr.). ~ QU
D6. The kind of utensils the modern bride has. ~ UNUSED
D7. Some crossword salt (sal) mixed with this makes delicious salads. ~ ADS
D8. As shrewd as they make them. ~ KEEN
D9. Pardon this mistake. ~ ERROR
D10. This is heavenly. ~ PLANET
D11. The last half of the detour. ~ OUR
D12. One way to get out of it. ~ SNAP
D13. This is no piece meal. ~ TABLEDHOTE
D18. Erases. ~ DELES
D22. This one is out for the big fish. ~ MINNOW
D24. This was quite a turn out. ~ OUST
D27. This was certainly a gift (such brilliancy). ~ NECKTIE
D29. This goes with a puff, puff, puff. ~ DAB
D30. So much has been written on this, it is not necessary to add any more. ~ STATIONERY
D31. This is very citified. ~ URBAN
D32. A short railway. ~ RY
D33. This sounds fishy. ~ ROE
D35. This has something to do with Schmeling. ~ NASAL
D39. Scoffs. ~ SNEERS
D43. A woman seldom makes a hit with this. ~ AIM
D46. Woman's Home Companion. ~ MAN
D47. To abound. ~ TEEM
D49. Harasses. ~ FRETS
D50. Autograph of a baseball. ~ BB
D52. The way two mosquitoes sound at night. ~ NEARER
D54. The salt of the sea. ~ SAILOR
D55. Don't give it up—stick to it. ~ ADHERE
D57. A doctrine (Dora might think it was a veterinary). ~ DOGMA
D60. This calls for one egg. ~ OVUM
D62. The kind of bars they now have in hotels. ~ SOAP
D64. This makes a very good appearance. ~ MIEN
D65. This one prefers to be single. ~ UNIT
D66. This makes a little change for the Jap cook. ~ SENS
D68. This is always found in delicatessens. (Yale men are very fond of it.) ~ ELI
D70. The way Ananias started. ~ ANA
D74. Absolutely right (abbr.). ~ RT


